      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "A strong argument against the \"meritocracy myth\" is that standardized tests often privilege specific cultural capital and dominant socioeconomic knowledge, favoring those with better early access to resources. Furthermore, the strong correlation between family wealth and high test scores suggests that \"merit\" often reflects pre-existing privilege more than inherent ability. In this view, these metrics are better indicators of class advantage than unbiased personal potential for success.",
